<div class="container">
    <div class="section">
        <h2>1. Basic Grid Structure</h2>
        <div class="explanation">
            <p>
                Bootstrap's grid consists of rows (<code>.row</code>) containing columns (<code>.col-*</code>).
                Each row divides into 12 columns. You can combine these columns to create different layouts.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-example">
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col">Single column (takes full width)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col">Column 1</div>
                <div class="col">Column 2</div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col">Column 1</div>
                <div class="col">Column 2</div>
                <div class="col">Column 3</div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col">Column 1</div>
                <div class="col">Column 2</div>
                <div class="col">Column 3</div>
                <div class="col">Column 4</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="container">
    <div class="section">
        <h2>2. Column Sizing with Numbers</h2>
        <div class="explanation">
            <p>
                You can explicitly define column widths using numbers (out of 12).
                For example, <code>col-6</code> takes up half of the row's width.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-example">
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col-12">col-12 (Full width)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col-6">col-6 (Half width)</div>
                <div class="col-6">col-6 (Half width)</div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col-4">col-4</div>
                <div class="col-4">col-4</div>
                <div class="col-4">col-4</div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col-3">col-3</div>
                <div class="col-3">col-3</div>
                <div class="col-3">col-3</div>
                <div class="col-3">col-3</div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col-8">col-8</div>
                <div class="col-4">col-4</div>
            </div>
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col-3">col-3</div>
                <div class="col-9">col-9</div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="container">
    <div class="section">
        <h2>3. Responsive Breakpoints</h2>
        <div class="explanation">
            <p>
                Bootstrap has 6 breakpoints: xs (< 576px), sm (≥ 576px), md (≥ 768px),
                lg (≥ 992px), xl (≥ 1200px), and xxl (≥ 1400px). Using these breakpoints,
                you can create responsive layouts.
            </p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-example">
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col-12 col-md-6 col-lg-4">
                    <p>Full width on xs, Half width on md, 1/3 width on lg</p>
                    <p><code>col-12 col-md-6 col-lg-4</code></p>
                </div>
                <div class="col-12 col-md-6 col-lg-4">
                    <p>Full width on xs, Half width on md, 1/3 width on lg</p>
                    <p><code>col-12 col-md-6 col-lg-4</code></p>
                </div>
                <div class="col-12 col-md-12 col-lg-4">
                    <p>Full width on xs, Full width on md, 1/3 width on lg</p>
                    <p><code>col-12 col-md-12 col-lg-4</code></p>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
        <div class="responsive-demo">
            <p><strong>Try it:</strong> Resize your browser window to see how the columns above change at different breakpoints!</p>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

<div class="container">
    <div class="section">
        <h2>4. Practical Layout Example</h2>
        <div class="explanation">
            <p>Here's a practical example of how you might structure a simple web page layout.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="grid-example">
            <div class="row">
                <div class="col-md-3">Sidebar (col-md-3)</div>
                <div class="col-md-9">
                    <div class="row mb-3">
                        <div class="col-12">Header Section (col-12)</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="row mb-3">
                        <div class="col-md-6">Content Section 1 (col-md-6)</div>
                        <div class="col-md-6">Content Section 2 (col-md-6)</div>
                    </div>
                    <div class="row">
                        <div class="col-12">Footer Section (col-12)</div>
                    </div>
                </div>
            </div>
        </div>
    </div>
</div>

